LECT o.*\nFROM orders AS o\nJOIN users  AS u ON u.id = o.user_id\nWHERE u.email = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\n1. **`SELECT *` returns columns from both tables.** Both tables probably have `id` and `created_at`, so the duplicate names can break clients that map rows by name. List the columns you need. If you only want order data, use `o.*` or an explicit list.\n\n2. **`lower(u.email)` stops an index on `email` from being used.** The planner can't use a plain index when the column is wrapped in a function, so it scans the whole `users` table. You have three options:\n   - Store emails already lowercased and compare directly, as above. This is the simplest fix.\n   - Add an expression index: `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));`. Keep `lower()` in the query.\n   - Use a case-insensitive type, such as `citext` in Postgres.\n\n   If your data isn't normalized and you keep `lower()`, also lowercase the literal. It's already lowercase here, but a hardcoded value could drift.\n\n3. **The email is hardcoded.** In application code, use a bound parameter (`$1` or `?`) instead of building the string. This avoids SQL injection and lets the database reuse the query plan.\n\n4. **Consider a lookup instead of a join.** If `users.email` is unique, a subquery does the same job and makes it clear that you're filtering orders by one user:\n   ```sql\n   SELECT o.* FROM orders o\n   WHERE o.user_id = (SELECT id FROM users WHERE email = $1);\n   ```\n   Make sure `orders.user_id` is indexed either way.\n\n5. **Add `ORDER BY` and `LIMIT` if this feeds a UI.** Without them, row order isn't guaranteed and a user with many orders returns everything.\n\nFor more rigorous analysis, tell me which database you use. I can then check the query plan or index suggestions against your real schema.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}